{
  "_comment": [
    "Model backends, as distinct from the tools that call them. A tool is not a provider:",
    "codex is a tool, OpenAI is a provider, and the interesting failures live in the gap.",
    "",
    "Detection is by SIGNAL, never by secret: whether an env var is set, never its value, and",
    "never by opening a credential file. `authEnv` names variables whose presence indicates a",
    "key is configured; `baseUrlEnv` names variables that REDIRECT the tool somewhere else,",
    "which is legitimate for a gateway and the highest-value thing to notice if it appeared",
    "without you setting it.",
    "",
    "See docs/registry.md for why this file can be updated from an untrusted source."
  ],
  "providers": [
    {
      "id": "anthropic",
      "name": "Anthropic",
      "authEnv": ["ANTHROPIC_API_KEY", "ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN"],
      "baseUrlEnv": ["ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL"],
      "oauthTools": ["claude"],
      "usedBy": ["claude"],
      "docs": "https://docs.claude.com/"
    },
    {
      "id": "openai",
      "name": "OpenAI",
      "authEnv": ["OPENAI_API_KEY"],
      "baseUrlEnv": ["OPENAI_BASE_URL"],
      "oauthTools": ["codex"],
      "usedBy": ["codex", "opencode", "aider", "continue"],
      "conflictNote": "codex supports both a ChatGPT login and an API key. When both are present the API KEY WINS, so usage bills to the key while the subscription sits idle. `codex doctor` reports this as 'mixed auth signals'.",
      "docs": "https://platform.openai.com/docs"
    },
    {
      "id": "xai",
      "name": "xAI",
      "authEnv": ["XAI_API_KEY", "GROK_API_KEY"],
      "baseUrlEnv": ["XAI_BASE_URL"],
      "oauthTools": ["grok"],
      "usedBy": ["grok"],
      "docs": "https://docs.x.ai/"
    },
    {
      "id": "google",
      "name": "Google (Gemini)",
      "authEnv": ["GEMINI_API_KEY", "GOOGLE_API_KEY", "GOOGLE_GENAI_API_KEY"],
      "baseUrlEnv": [],
      "oauthTools": ["gemini"],
      "usedBy": ["gemini", "antigravity"],
      "conflictNote": "gemini supports a Google account login as well as an API key. GOOGLE_API_KEY is broad — it is used by many non-Gemini Google services, so its presence is weaker evidence than GEMINI_API_KEY.",
      "docs": "https://ai.google.dev/"
    },
    {
      "id": "moonshot",
      "name": "Moonshot (Kimi)",
      "authEnv": ["MOONSHOT_API_KEY", "KIMI_API_KEY"],
